  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a neural circuit normally develops and how that process is disrupted in RTT. A timeline across the top places these changes from early development through maturation, circuit refinement, and mature circuit maintenance, with activity-dependent plasticity spanning the middle stages.\n\nIn the WT condition, a simple immature green neuron differentiates into a more elaborate cell and then becomes integrated into a balanced mature network. That mature circuit includes inhibitory interneuron types labeled SOM and PV, plus VIP input influenced by neuromodulators. The layout suggests coordinated inhibitory control and stable connectivity around the principal green neuron.\n\nIn contrast, the RTT row shows the same neuron progressing abnormally. The early step is marked as impaired differentiation, producing a less developed cell, followed by impaired maturation. The final circuit is drawn with dashed, altered connections and a note indicating reduced neuromodulators. Compared with WT, the inhibitory network involving SOM, PV, and VIP appears weakened or disorganized, and the principal neuron remains structurally simplified.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that RTT disrupts normal neuronal differentiation, maturation, and neuromodulator-dependent circuit refinement, leading to altered connectivity and dysfunctional mature networks.",
